
; ======================================================================
;  hw_addr.pi4 - the Pi 4's answer to "is this address safe to touch".
;
;  The core asks HwAddrCheck() before every command that takes an address
;  from the operator (Anvil/Core/addrsafe.pbi, and the contract in
;  Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi). ON THIS BOARD EVERY ADDRESS THAT EXISTS ANSWERS
;  SAFE, and this file exists to say so deliberately rather than by
;  omission. THE ONE REFUSAL IS FOR ADDRESSES THAT DO NOT EXIST: a Pi 4
;  is sold with 1, 2, 4 or 8 GB fitted, the monitor is the same image on
;  all of them, and until 2026-09-17 it had no idea which one it was
;  running on - so `memory 80000000` on a 1 GB board read an address
;  with no memory behind it and printed the result as though it were
;  data. See THE TOP OF MEMORY below.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  WHY SAFE FOR EVERYTHING THAT IS THERE, AND WHAT THAT CLAIM IS AND IS NOT
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The seam exists because on the Arduino UNO Q's QCM2290 an MMIO access
;  to a clock-gated block STALLS THE INTERCONNECT - the instruction never
;  retires and the board needs its power removing. THE BCM2711 DOES NOT
;  BEHAVE THAT WAY, and that is a measured difference and not an
;  assumption about it:
;
;    * Every peripheral in the $FE000000 window is behind clocks the
;      firmware has already started before Anvil is entered. `memory
;      FE201000 20` reading the PL011 is how the mini-UART routing bug
;      was cornered, and that read is in this monitor's own history as a
;      thing that WORKED - see the note at CmdMem in Anvil/Core/memcmd.pbi.
;    * The V3D, DMA, mailbox, GENET, PCIe/xHCI, EMMC and SDIO register
;      blocks are all read by this monitor's own drivers, at this
;      exception level, with no special preparation beyond what those
;      drivers do. The register windows answer.
;    * DRAM answers everywhere it is fitted. `mtest` writes and reads it
;      back. "Everywhere" used to be written here without the last three
;      words, and that is the sentence THE TOP OF MEMORY below repairs.
;    * An address with NOTHING behind it reads as a value and does not
;      hang. It is a wrong answer rather than a hang, and the monitor has
;      always said out loud that reading such an address is the
;      operator's risk to take.
;
;  SO THE CLAIM IS NARROW AND IT IS TRUE: no address on this board is
;  known to stall the bus. It is NOT the claim that every address holds
;  something useful, and it is NOT the claim that a write anywhere is a
;  good idea - the monitor's own window refusals (HitsMonitor,
;  InPayload) are unchanged and still run, because they answer a
;  completely different question: not "will this access complete" but
;  "will it destroy the thing that is running".
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  WHY THIS IS A FILE AND NOT THREE LINES AT THE FOOT OF THE MEMORY MAP
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Two reasons, and the second is the load-bearing one.
;
;  It needs #HW_ADDR_SAFE, which is defined in Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi, and
;  RaspberryPi4/Board/memmap.pi4 is included BEFORE hal.pi4 by
;  RaspberryPi4/Examples/Diagnostics/pi4BootContainerSelfTest.pi4. Putting
;  the answer in the memory map would break that diagnostic's build for
;  no reason at all.
;
;  And a board that answers "everything is fine" should have to write
;  that down somewhere a reader will find it. An empty implementation is
;  indistinguishable from a forgotten one, and the next person to bring
;  up a third target will read this file to find out what the seam is
;  for. If the Pi 4 ever grows a block that has to be voted for - a
;  power-domain the firmware leaves collapsed, say - the table goes here
;  and nothing else in the tree changes.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE TOP OF MEMORY - the one thing this board refuses. 2026-09-17.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The instruction that started this work, 2026-09-17: "I think anvil
;  should be smart enough to know how much ram its working with." It now
;  is - RaspberryPi4/Board/hw_mem.pi4 decodes the size out of the
;  firmware's board revision code - and the first thing worth doing with
;  the number is refusing to read past it.
;
;  WHAT AN ADDRESS ABOVE THE MEMORY ACTUALLY DOES HERE, because the
;  refusal has to be honest about which kind of refusal it is. It does
;  NOT stall the bus; nothing on this board does. It reads back whatever
;  the interconnect leaves on the wire, which is a plausible-looking
;  word, printed in a plausible-looking dump, under a heading that says
;  it is the contents of memory. That is the worst failure this monitor
;  has: not a hang, which announces itself, but a wrong answer that
;  looks exactly like a right one. So the answer is #HW_ADDR_ABSENT -
;  the fourth answer, added with this work, which says the board KNOWS
;  there is nothing here rather than that it is afraid of the address.
;  It is overridable by `force` like every other refusal, because a
;  debugger that cannot be pointed at an address its board calls empty
;  cannot prove the board wrong.
;
;  WHERE THE MEMORY IS, and it is not one range. The BCM2711 ARM
;  Peripherals document, 1.2.3 "ARM physical addresses": "The ARM
;  section of the RAM starts at 0x0_0000_0000 and extends up to the size
;  of installed SDRAM." Its Figure 1, the "ARM view of the Address Map
;  in Low Peripheral mode" - which is the mode this board boots in -
;  shows that run of SDRAM INTERRUPTED at $FC000000 by the main
;  peripherals and the ARM-local peripherals, and resuming at
;  $1_0000_0000. So the memory a 4 GB board has is the whole of
;  $00000000..$FBFFFFFF plus the 64 MB the peripheral window displaced,
;  which reappears at $1_0000_0000; an 8 GB board's second run is
;  correspondingly longer; and a 1 GB or 2 GB board has no second run at
;  all, because all of its memory fits below the peripherals.
;
;  AND THIS MONITOR CANNOT REACH THE SECOND RUN. Found on silicon,
;  build 183, 2026-09-18, by typing the very address the paragraph above
;  had just invited an operator to type:
;
;      memory 103FFFFC0 40
;      !! Anvil stopped after a processor exception.
;         EL=3 slot=4 ESR=$96000005 PC=$00367E94 FAR=$103FFFFC0
;
;  ESR $96000005 is a data abort, level-1 translation fault. The memory
;  is there in the silicon; this monitor's PAGE TABLES do not map it.
;  RaspberryPi4/Lib/mmu.pi4's MmuBuildTables fills sixty-four 1 GiB
;  level-1 slots and leaves every slot between the low four gigabytes
;  and the PCIe outbound window at #MMU_DESC_FAULT - deliberately, and
;  it always has. So exactly two spans translate on this board:
;
;      $0_00000000..$0_FFFFFFFF   slots 0..3: DRAM, then the peripheral
;                                 and ARM-local apertures
;      $6_00000000..$7_FFFFFFFF   slots #MMU_PCIE_SLOT_LO.._HI: the
;                                 PCIe outbound window
;
;  and everything else faults. A FAULT IS WORSE THAN A WRONG VALUE:
;  Anvil prints its fatal report and stops, the console dies with it,
;  and with no deadman armed the board has to have its power removed by
;  hand. That is the outcome #HW_ADDR_UNCLOCKED's refusal describes word
;  for word, so that is the answer this file gives for an unmapped
;  address - and it is given BEFORE the memory-size rules and without
;  asking the size at all, because the fault does not care how much
;  memory is fitted or whether the board could say.
;
;  THE ORDER IS THEREFORE: unmapped first, then absent, then safe. The
;  UNO Q's table next door has carried "dangerous first, then safe" from
;  the day it was written; this board now has something dangerous too.
;
;  THE ARITHMETIC THAT IS LEFT IS ONE EDGE, NOT TWO:
;
;    lowTop  = the smaller of the size and $FC000000. Everything from
;              lowTop up to the peripheral window is memory that is not
;              there - mapped, so it reads a plausible value, which is
;              why it is refused as ABSENT and not as a fault.
;
;  The second run needs no arithmetic any more: every byte of it is
;  inside the unmapped span, and it is refused for the stronger and
;  truer reason before the size is ever consulted.
;
;  IT BORROWS #MMU_PERIPH_BASE, #MMU_PCIE_SLOT_LO AND #MMU_PCIE_SLOT_HI
;  FROM RaspberryPi4/Lib/mmu.pi4 rather than writing the addresses
;  again. Those are the numbers the page tables are actually built from,
;  and this file's whole claim is about what those tables map: a second
;  copy of them is a second thing to keep in step, and the first time it
;  drifted the monitor would stop on an address it had called safe.
;
;  WHEN THE SIZE IS NOT KNOWN, NOTHING IS REFUSED FOR BEING PAST IT.
;  HwMemoryBytes() answers -1 if the mailbox is down or the revision
;  code is old-style, and the honest response to "I do not know where
;  memory ends" is the behaviour this board had yesterday. The unmapped
;  refusal is unaffected: it never asked.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The edges above that are not in the memory map file.
;
;  $1_0000_0000 is where Figure 1 resumes SDRAM above the peripheral
;  aperture; $4_0000_0000 is where that region ends. They are here and
;  not in RaspberryPi4/Board/memmap.pi4 for the reason the section above
;  this one gives about #HW_ADDR_SAFE: that file is included BEFORE
;  hal.pi4 by a diagnostic, and the whole of this answer - the codes,
;  the sentences and these two edges - belongs to one question that is
;  asked in one place.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHERE THE LOW MAPPED SPAN ENDS AND THE UNMAPPED ONE BEGINS. $1_0000_0000
; is both the first address above level-1 slot 3 and the address at which
; Figure 1 resumes SDRAM; the two coincide, and the coincidence is why the
; memory above four gigabytes is real and unreachable at the same time.
#PI_DRAM_HIGH_BASE  = $100000000

; THE PCIe OUTBOUND WINDOW, THE ONLY OTHER THING THE TABLES MAP. These are
; mmu.pi4's #MMU_PCIE_SLOT_LO and #MMU_PCIE_SLOT_HI turned into addresses -
; slot 24 is $6_0000_0000 and slot 31 ends at $7_FFFFFFFF - written out
; because a constant initialiser in this compiler takes no arithmetic and
; `#MMU_PCIE_SLOT_LO << 30` cannot be written here. The gate recomputes both
; from the slot numbers, so the two cannot drift apart in silence.
#PI_PCIE_WINDOW_LO  = $600000000
#PI_PCIE_WINDOW_HI  = $7FFFFFFFF

; THE TOP OF WHAT THE TRANSLATION REGIME COVERS AT ALL. mmu.pi4 builds 64
; level-1 slots of a gigabyte each, so the tables span 64 GiB and an address
; above this one is outside translation entirely rather than merely landing
; on a faulting descriptor. Both stop the processor; only the sentence would
; differ, so one span covers to here and the last arm catches the rest.
#PI_VA_TOP          = $FFFFFFFFF

; The block the last check matched, and the sentence naming it. Valid
; only immediately after a HwAddrCheck() that did not answer SAFE - the
; same rule, and the same reason, as memrange.pi4's gMonHitLo/gMonHitHi.
; They were three procedures returning 0 until 2026-09-17, when this
; board acquired something to refuse.
Global gPiAddrLo.i
Global gPiAddrHi.i
Global *gPiAddrWhy

Procedure.i HwAddrReason()
  ProcedureReturn *gPiAddrWhy
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwAddrBlockLo()
  ProcedureReturn gPiAddrLo
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwAddrBlockHi()
  ProcedureReturn gPiAddrHi
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  PiAddrHit - does lo..hi touch blo..bhi AT ALL, and record it if so.
;
;  ANY OVERLAP COUNTS. A dump that starts four bytes below the top of
;  memory still reads past it, and the bytes that are not there are the
;  ones inside. ArduinoQ/Board/hw_addr_q.unoq's QAddrHit is the same
;  shape for the same reason, and so is the monitor-overlap check next
;  door in memrange.pi4.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i PiAddrHit(lo.i, hi.i, blo.i, bhi.i)
  If hi >= blo And lo <= bhi
    gPiAddrLo = blo
    gPiAddrHi = bhi
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  PiMemTooHighWhy(bytes) - the refusal's sentence, naming the size.
;
;  It reads after "is " in Anvil/Core/addrsafe.pbi's #HW_ADDR_ABSENT
;  refusal: "is above the 4 GB of RAM this board has".
;
;  ONE WHOLE SENTENCE PER SIZE, and not a number assembled at the point
;  of printing, because HwAddrReason() returns the ADDRESS of a fragment
;  - that is the seam's shape on both boards - and a fragment built into
;  a buffer would need somebody to own the buffer. Six sizes is the
;  whole of what the MEMSIZE field means today, and the last arm catches
;  the board that ships a seventh: it still refuses, and it still says
;  why, without naming a size this monitor would be inventing.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i PiMemTooHighWhy(bytes.i)
  If bytes = $10000000
    ProcedureReturn "above the 256 MB of RAM this board has"
  ElseIf bytes = $20000000
    ProcedureReturn "above the 512 MB of RAM this board has"
  ElseIf bytes = $40000000
    ProcedureReturn "above the 1 GB of RAM this board has"
  ElseIf bytes = $80000000
    ProcedureReturn "above the 2 GB of RAM this board has"
  ElseIf bytes = $100000000
    ProcedureReturn "above the 4 GB of RAM this board has"
  ElseIf bytes = $200000000
    ProcedureReturn "above the 8 GB of RAM this board has"
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn "above the RAM this board has, which is a size this monitor has no words for - type info for the byte count"
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwAddrCheck(lo, hi, forWrite) - the Pi 4's answer.
;
;  forWrite IS ACCEPTED AND NOT USED, and that is honest rather than
;  lazy: an address above the memory this board has is no more readable
;  than it is writable, and there is no block on this board that may be
;  read but not written. It stays in the signature because the seam is
;  shared and a board that DOES have such a block needs somewhere to say
;  so. Naming it also keeps the signature identical across boards, which
;  is what makes a backend a swap rather than a rewrite.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwAddrCheck(lo.i, hi.i, forWrite.i)
  Define bytes.i
  Define lowTop.i

  *gPiAddrWhy = 0
  gPiAddrLo = 0
  gPiAddrHi = 0

  ; ==================================================================
  ;  1. NOT MAPPED BY THIS MONITOR. Any overlap refuses the whole range,
  ;     and the size is not consulted: a translation fault stops the
  ;     board whatever is or is not fitted. See AND THIS MONITOR CANNOT
  ;     REACH THE SECOND RUN in the header - this arm exists because an
  ;     operator typed one of these addresses on build 183 and the
  ;     monitor stopped on it.
  ; ==================================================================
  If lo < 0 Or hi < 0
    *gPiAddrWhy = "past the top of the address space this monitor translates at all: an access there is a fault and Anvil stops on it"
    gPiAddrLo = 0
    gPiAddrHi = 0
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNCLOCKED
  EndIf
  If PiAddrHit(lo, hi, #PI_DRAM_HIGH_BASE, #PI_PCIE_WINDOW_LO - 1) <> 0
    *gPiAddrWhy = "the span above four gigabytes that this monitor's page tables leave unmapped: any memory a 4 or 8 GB board has up here is real and out of this monitor's reach, and an access to it is a translation fault that Anvil stops on"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNCLOCKED
  EndIf
  If PiAddrHit(lo, hi, #PI_PCIE_WINDOW_HI + 1, #PI_VA_TOP) <> 0
    *gPiAddrWhy = "above the PCI Express window, where this monitor's page tables map nothing at all: an access there is a translation fault that Anvil stops on"
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNCLOCKED
  EndIf
  If hi > #PI_VA_TOP Or lo > #PI_VA_TOP
    *gPiAddrWhy = "past the top of the address space this monitor translates at all: an access there is a fault and Anvil stops on it"
    gPiAddrLo = 0
    gPiAddrHi = 0
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_UNCLOCKED
  EndIf

  ; ==================================================================
  ;  2. MAPPED, BUT THERE IS NO MEMORY BEHIND IT. Only the run below the
  ;     peripheral aperture can be in this state: the run above four
  ;     gigabytes was refused by arm 1 whether or not it holds memory.
  ; ==================================================================
  bytes = HwMemoryBytes()
  If bytes = #HW_MEM_UNKNOWN
    ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_SAFE
  EndIf
  lowTop = bytes
  If lowTop > #MMU_PERIPH_BASE
    lowTop = #MMU_PERIPH_BASE
  EndIf
  If lowTop < #MMU_PERIPH_BASE
    If PiAddrHit(lo, hi, lowTop, #MMU_PERIPH_BASE - 1) <> 0
      *gPiAddrWhy = PiMemTooHighWhy(bytes)
      ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_ABSENT
    EndIf
  EndIf

  ProcedureReturn #HW_ADDR_SAFE
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwAddrReport() - what `info` prints about this board's answer.
;
;  Every board prints its own table here, with where each row came from.
;  This one has no table, so it says that, and says why - a blank section
;  under a heading in `info` reads as a monitor that failed to fill it in.
;
;  AND IT PRINTS WHERE THE MONITOR IS, because the sentence below sends
;  the reader straight to "the monitor's own window guards" and there was
;  no way to ask what those windows are. Two questions meet in this
;  section and they are not the same one: HwAddrCheck answers "will this
;  access complete" - here, always yes - and the monitor window answers
;  "will this access destroy the thing that is running", which is the one
;  that still refuses writes on this board. The extent was reachable only
;  by tripping over it: aim a write inside the monitor and the refusal
;  names the window. A number an operator can only find by hitting it is
;  not reported, it is hidden.
;
;  IT ASKS HwMonLo()/HwMonHi() RATHER THAN READING THE CONSTANTS, even
;  though on this board they ARE the constants. Those two procedures are
;  what the overlap test itself asks, so this prints the answer that will
;  actually be used - and a board that told `info` one thing and the
;  guard another would then have to be made to disagree on purpose,
;  rather than by an oversight in one of them.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure HwAddrReport()
  ; WHERE THE MONITOR IS. First, because it is the concrete answer and
  ; the prose below it explains a refusal that never happens here.
  ; EVERY REGION, THROUGH THE ONE PRINTER THE MAP HAS - 2026-09-08.
  ; This block used to print the code window and the data window from
  ; constants and then say the pair "come from COMPILE-TIME CONSTANTS on
  ; this board and cannot be wrong". That sentence was true of the data
  ; window and FALSE of the code window from the moment the monitor
  ; stopped having a fixed top: its extent is now the size of the image
  ; the firmware loaded, measured by the running program. A section of
  ; `info` that explained the old certainty would have been the most
  ; confident wrong paragraph in the monitor.
  PutMonitorMap()
  PrintN("            A write into any of that is refused and the refusal names")
  PrintN("            the region. The first line is MEASURED, not declared: the")
  PrintN("            image carries the address of its own end, so the monitor")
  PrintN("            reserves exactly what it is and there is no maximum size.")
  PrintN("            Type map for the same numbers with the payload windows.")
  PrintN("addresses   every address that exists on this board answers SAFE, so")
  PrintN("            the commands that take an address from you are refused by")
  PrintN("            nothing but the monitor's own window guards. Nothing here")
  PrintN("            is known to stall the bus: the peripheral window is")
  PrintN("            clocked by the firmware before this monitor is entered,")
  PrintN("            and an address with nothing behind it reads a value")
  PrintN("            rather than hanging. Anvil asks the question because its")
  PrintN("            other target is not like this - there, one read of a")
  PrintN("            gated block stops the processor until the power is")
  PrintN("            pulled - and one core asks every board the same thing.")

  ; THE ONE REFUSAL, AND THE SHAPE OF THE MEMORY BEHIND IT. NO SIZE IS
  ; PRINTED HERE, on purpose: this section is printed before the mailbox
  ; is asked for anything (see CmdInfo), and the size comes from the
  ; firmware. The RAM row a few lines further down `info` carries the
  ; number and where it came from. What belongs here is the SHAPE -
  ; memory, then the peripheral aperture, then memory again - because
  ; that is the reason an address that looks like it is inside a 4 GB
  ; board's memory is not, and an operator is entitled to know it before
  ; typing rather than after being refused.
  PrintN("memory top  two things are refused here and they are different. An")
  PrintN("            address past the memory this board has, but below the")
  PrintN("            peripheral aperture, reads a plausible value rather than")
  PrintN("            hanging - a wrong answer that looks like a right one is")
  PrintN("            worse than a stop - so it is refused with the size named.")
  PrintN("            And EVERYTHING ABOVE FOUR GIGABYTES except the PCI")
  PrintN("            Express window is refused for a harder reason: this")
  PrintN("            monitor's page tables map nothing there, so an access is")
  PrintN("            a translation fault and Anvil stops on it. Memory does")
  PrintN("            resume up there on a 4 or 8 GB board and this monitor")
  PrintN("            still cannot reach it. force overrides either refusal for")
  PrintN("            one address and one command line, as it does any other -")
  PrintN("            and forcing the second one will stop the board.")
EndProcedure
